      "title": "Infinitely many locations need not mean infinite mass",
      "html": "<figure class=\"volume7-explain\" id=\"visual-V49\" data-v7=\"V49\" lang=\"en\"><header><span>V49</span><h3>Infinitely many locations need not mean infinite mass</h3></header><p>The same infinite set of locations can carry finite mass or overload one compact window. Choose the space before the window.</p><div class=\"v7-controls\" role=\"group\"><button type=\"button\" data-space=\"real\" aria-pressed=\"true\">E = R</button><button type=\"button\" data-space=\"open\" aria-pressed=\"false\">E = (0,1)</button></div><div class=\"v7-controls\" role=\"group\"><button type=\"button\" data-window=\"0\" aria-pressed=\"true\">K = [1/4,3/4]</button><button type=\"button\" data-window=\"1\" aria-pressed=\"false\">K = [1/16,3/4]</button><button type=\"button\" data-window=\"2\" aria-pressed=\"false\">K = [0,3/4]</button></div><picture data-v7-picture><source media=\"(max-width:700px)\" srcset=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V49-en-real-0-mobile.svg\"><img class=\"v7-scene\" src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V49-en-real-0-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Infinitely many locations need not mean infinite mass\" loading=\"eager\"></picture><p class=\"v7-boundary\">Total mass: mu=1, eta=infinity; dx is infinite on R and 1 on (0,1).</p><div class=\"v7-print\"><section><h4>Infinitely many locations need not mean infinite mass · 1/2</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V49.en.page1.svg\" alt=\"Infinitely many locations need not mean infinite mass\"><p class=\"v7-print-caption\">Let x_k=1/(k+1), k&gt;=1. Compare mu=sum 2^(-k) delta_(x_k), eta=sum delta_(x_k), and Lebesgue measure. The first two have the same infinitely many locations, but total masses 1 and infinity. On R, a compact window containing 0 makes eta locally infinite; mu remains Radon. On E=(0,1), eta is Radon because compact subsets stay away from 0. The displayed closed interval containing 0 is not a compact subset of E: a dash does not mean zero mass. [1/4,3/4] contains 3 atoms with mu mass 7/8; [1/16,3/4] contains 15 with mass 1-2^(-15). Circle areas encode initial weights; the ellipsis stands for the unpictured tail. Infinite claims follow from the full series and compact-set argument, not from drawn point counts.</p></section><section><h4>Infinitely many locations need not mean infinite mass · 2/2</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V49.en.page2.svg\" alt=\"Infinitely many locations need not mean infinite mass\"><p class=\"v7-print-caption\">Let x_k=1/(k+1), k&gt;=1. Compare mu=sum 2^(-k) delta_(x_k), eta=sum delta_(x_k), and Lebesgue measure. The first two have the same infinitely many locations, but total masses 1 and infinity. On R, a compact window containing 0 makes eta locally infinite; mu remains Radon. On E=(0,1), eta is Radon because compact subsets stay away from 0. The displayed closed interval containing 0 is not a compact subset of E: a dash does not mean zero mass. [1/4,3/4] contains 3 atoms with mu mass 7/8; [1/16,3/4] contains 15 with mass 1-2^(-15). Circle areas encode initial weights; the ellipsis stands for the unpictured tail. Infinite claims follow from the full series and compact-set argument, not from drawn point counts.</p></section></div><output data-v7-result aria-live=\"polite\">In the window: weighted mass 7/8, unit count 3, length 1/2.</output><details><summary>Assumptions and diagram limits</summary><p>Let x_k=1/(k+1), k&gt;=1. Compare mu=sum 2^(-k) delta_(x_k), eta=sum delta_(x_k), and Lebesgue measure. The first two have the same infinitely many locations, but total masses 1 and infinity. On R, a compact window containing 0 makes eta locally infinite; mu remains Radon. On E=(0,1), eta is Radon because compact subsets stay away from 0. The displayed closed interval containing 0 is not a compact subset of E: a dash does not mean zero mass. [1/4,3/4] contains 3 atoms with mu mass 7/8; [1/16,3/4] contains 15 with mass 1-2^(-15). Circle areas encode initial weights; the ellipsis stands for the unpictured tail. Infinite claims follow from the full series and compact-set argument, not from drawn point counts.</p></details><figcaption>An exact finite model reveals the mechanism; full theorems and proofs remain in the text.</figcaption><nav><a href=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V49.en.svg\">SVG</a><a href=\"/print/figures/V49.en.html\">Static print</a></nav></figure>",
