      "title": "Disjoint windows can be hit by the same event",
      "html": "<figure class=\"volume7-explain\" id=\"visual-V51\" data-v7=\"V51\" lang=\"en\"><header><span>V51</span><h3>Disjoint windows can be hit by the same event</h3></header><p>The two vertical windows never touch. Can one random horizontal line hit both?</p><div class=\"v7-controls\" role=\"group\"><button type=\"button\" data-case=\"0\" aria-pressed=\"true\">Same height interval</button><button type=\"button\" data-case=\"1\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Separated heights</button><button type=\"button\" data-case=\"2\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Partly overlapping heights</button></div><label class=\"v7-range\">U <input type=\"range\" min=\"0\" max=\"20\" step=\"1\" value=\"10\" data-height aria-label=\"Illustrative line height\"><span data-height-label>1/2</span></label><picture data-v7-picture><source media=\"(max-width:700px)\" srcset=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V51-en-0-10-mobile.svg\"><img class=\"v7-scene\" src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V51-en-0-10-desktop.svg\" alt=\"Disjoint windows can be hit by the same event\" loading=\"eager\"></picture><div class=\"v7-print\"><section><h4>Disjoint windows can be hit by the same event · 1/3</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V51.en.page1.svg\" alt=\"Disjoint windows can be hit by the same event\"><p class=\"v7-print-caption\">U is continuously uniform on [0,1], and F_U=[0,1] x {U}. The two compact vertical segments have different horizontal coordinates, so they are disjoint in space. Each is hit exactly when U is in its closed height interval. T(K) is the length of the height projection; the probability of hitting both is T(K_a)+T(K_b)-T(K_a union K_b). Readings use continuous Lebesgue probability, not a discrete uniform law on slider positions. Endpoints are included but have probability zero individually. This shows nonadditivity and recovery of joint readings from unions; the general LCSC Choquet theorem and complete alternation remain the responsibility of the text.</p></section><section><h4>Disjoint windows can be hit by the same event · 2/3</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V51.en.page2.svg\" alt=\"Disjoint windows can be hit by the same event\"><p class=\"v7-print-caption\">U is continuously uniform on [0,1], and F_U=[0,1] x {U}. The two compact vertical segments have different horizontal coordinates, so they are disjoint in space. Each is hit exactly when U is in its closed height interval. T(K) is the length of the height projection; the probability of hitting both is T(K_a)+T(K_b)-T(K_a union K_b). Readings use continuous Lebesgue probability, not a discrete uniform law on slider positions. Endpoints are included but have probability zero individually. This shows nonadditivity and recovery of joint readings from unions; the general LCSC Choquet theorem and complete alternation remain the responsibility of the text.</p></section><section><h4>Disjoint windows can be hit by the same event · 3/3</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V51.en.page3.svg\" alt=\"Disjoint windows can be hit by the same event\"><p class=\"v7-print-caption\">U is continuously uniform on [0,1], and F_U=[0,1] x {U}. The two compact vertical segments have different horizontal coordinates, so they are disjoint in space. Each is hit exactly when U is in its closed height interval. T(K) is the length of the height projection; the probability of hitting both is T(K_a)+T(K_b)-T(K_a union K_b). Readings use continuous Lebesgue probability, not a discrete uniform law on slider positions. Endpoints are included but have probability zero individually. This shows nonadditivity and recovery of joint readings from unions; the general LCSC Choquet theorem and complete alternation remain the responsibility of the text.</p></section></div><output data-v7-result aria-live=\"polite\">T(K_a)=2/5; T(K_b)=2/5; T(union)=2/5; both hit=2/5.</output><details><summary>Assumptions and diagram limits</summary><p>U is continuously uniform on [0,1], and F_U=[0,1] x {U}. The two compact vertical segments have different horizontal coordinates, so they are disjoint in space. Each is hit exactly when U is in its closed height interval. T(K) is the length of the height projection; the probability of hitting both is T(K_a)+T(K_b)-T(K_a union K_b). Readings use continuous Lebesgue probability, not a discrete uniform law on slider positions. Endpoints are included but have probability zero individually. This shows nonadditivity and recovery of joint readings from unions; the general LCSC Choquet theorem and complete alternation remain the responsibility of the text.</p></details><figcaption>An exact finite model reveals the mechanism; full theorems and proofs remain in the text.</figcaption><nav><a href=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V51.en.svg\">SVG</a><a href=\"/print/figures/V51.en.html\">Static print</a></nav></figure>",
