      "title": "One tree, three records and two clocks",
      "html": "<figure class=\"volume7-explain\" id=\"visual-V52\" data-v7=\"V52\" lang=\"en\"><header><span>V52</span><h3>One tree, three records and two clocks</h3></header><p>Choose a vertex, then locate it in the pending list, preorder height and edge contour.</p><div class=\"v7-controls\" role=\"group\"><button type=\"button\" data-tree=\"A\" aria-pressed=\"true\">Tree A: the last generation are siblings</button><button type=\"button\" data-tree=\"B\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Tree B: different parents</button></div><div class=\"v7-controls\" role=\"group\"><button type=\"button\" data-vertex=\"0\" aria-pressed=\"false\">j = 0</button><button type=\"button\" data-vertex=\"1\" aria-pressed=\"false\">j = 1</button><button type=\"button\" data-vertex=\"2\" aria-pressed=\"false\">j = 2</button><button type=\"button\" data-vertex=\"3\" aria-pressed=\"true\">j = 3</button><button type=\"button\" data-vertex=\"4\" aria-pressed=\"false\">j = 4</button></div><picture data-v7-picture><source media=\"(max-width:700px)\" srcset=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V52-en-A-3-mobile.svg\"><img class=\"v7-scene\" src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V52-en-A-3-desktop.svg\" alt=\"One tree, three records and two clocks\" loading=\"eager\"></picture><div class=\"v7-print\"><section><h4>One tree, three records and two clocks · 1/2</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V52.en.page1.svg\" alt=\"One tree, three records and two clocks\"><p class=\"v7-print-caption\">These are exactly the two rooted plane trees of five vertices in section 52.1. Order is depth-first preorder; new children enter the front of the task list. Q_j=1+S_j counts pending positions before vertex j, while H_j is its depth. S has 5 increments and 6 endpoints; H has 5 first-visit readings; C traverses each edge twice and lasts 2(n-1)=8. Its first visit to vertex j is b_j=2j-H_j. Neither values nor clocks are identified. Both generation profiles are 1,2,2, but encodings differ. S_5=-1 means an empty task list, not negative population. No sixth vertex is added to H. A finite bijection is not a tree limit theorem or an enumeration theorem for arbitrary infinite trees.</p></section><section><h4>One tree, three records and two clocks · 2/2</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V52.en.page2.svg\" alt=\"One tree, three records and two clocks\"><p class=\"v7-print-caption\">These are exactly the two rooted plane trees of five vertices in section 52.1. Order is depth-first preorder; new children enter the front of the task list. Q_j=1+S_j counts pending positions before vertex j, while H_j is its depth. S has 5 increments and 6 endpoints; H has 5 first-visit readings; C traverses each edge twice and lasts 2(n-1)=8. Its first visit to vertex j is b_j=2j-H_j. Neither values nor clocks are identified. Both generation profiles are 1,2,2, but encodings differ. S_5=-1 means an empty task list, not negative population. No sixth vertex is added to H. A finite bijection is not a tree limit theorem or an enumeration theorem for arbitrary infinite trees.</p></section></div><output data-v7-result aria-live=\"polite\">j=3: S=1, Q=2, H=2, b=4; C(4)=2.</output><details><summary>Assumptions and diagram limits</summary><p>These are exactly the two rooted plane trees of five vertices in section 52.1. Order is depth-first preorder; new children enter the front of the task list. Q_j=1+S_j counts pending positions before vertex j, while H_j is its depth. S has 5 increments and 6 endpoints; H has 5 first-visit readings; C traverses each edge twice and lasts 2(n-1)=8. Its first visit to vertex j is b_j=2j-H_j. Neither values nor clocks are identified. Both generation profiles are 1,2,2, but encodings differ. S_5=-1 means an empty task list, not negative population. No sixth vertex is added to H. A finite bijection is not a tree limit theorem or an enumeration theorem for arbitrary infinite trees.</p></details><figcaption>An exact finite model reveals the mechanism; full theorems and proofs remain in the text.</figcaption><nav><a href=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V52.en.svg\">SVG</a><a href=\"/print/figures/V52.en.html\">Static print</a></nav></figure>",
      "condition": "These are exactly the two rooted plane trees of five vertices in section 52.1. Order is depth-first preorder; new children enter the front of the task list. Q_j=1+S_j counts pending positions before vertex j, while H_j is its depth. S has 5 increments and 6 endpoints; H has 5 first-visit readings; C traverses each edge twice and lasts 2(n-1)=8. Its first visit to vertex j is b_j=2j-H_j. Neither values nor clocks are identified. Both generation profiles are 1,2,2, but encodings differ. S_5=-1 means an empty task list, not negative population. No sixth vertex is added to H. A finite bijection is not a tree limit theorem or an enumeration theorem for arbitrary infinite trees.",
