      "title": "A legal cut does not yet control height",
      "html": "<figure class=\"volume7-explain\" id=\"visual-V53\" data-v7=\"V53\" lang=\"en\"><header><span>V53</span><h3>A legal cut does not yet control height</h3></header><p>Do the first and last minima of the same increment word give equally legal tree words?</p><div class=\"v7-controls\" role=\"group\"><button type=\"button\" data-cut=\"0\" aria-pressed=\"false\">r=0</button><button type=\"button\" data-cut=\"1\" aria-pressed=\"true\">r=1 first min</button><button type=\"button\" data-cut=\"2\" aria-pressed=\"false\">r=2</button><button type=\"button\" data-cut=\"3\" aria-pressed=\"false\">r=3 last min</button><button type=\"button\" data-cut=\"4\" aria-pressed=\"false\">r=4</button></div><picture data-v7-picture><source media=\"(max-width:700px)\" srcset=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V53-en-1-mobile.svg\"><img class=\"v7-scene\" src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V53-en-1-desktop.svg\" alt=\"A legal cut does not yet control height\" loading=\"eager\"></picture><details class=\"v7-deeper\"><summary>Why does height still need a separate argument?</summary><p>Prepend a unary ancestral chain: each new layer adds a zero task increment but one level of ancestry.</p><div class=\"v7-controls\" role=\"group\"><button type=\"button\" data-chain=\"0\" aria-pressed=\"false\">ℓ = 0</button><button type=\"button\" data-chain=\"2\" aria-pressed=\"true\">ℓ = 2</button><button type=\"button\" data-chain=\"5\" aria-pressed=\"false\">ℓ = 5</button></div><img class=\"v7-chain\" data-chain-scene src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V53-en-chain-2.svg\" alt=\"Height and task readings of a unary chain\"><p>l_m=floor(m^(3/4)): relative time vanishes, but the height on a square-root scale diverges. This is a deterministic counterexample, not a fixed conditioned-GW sample.</p></details><div class=\"v7-print\"><section><h4>A legal cut does not yet control height · 1/3</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V53.en.page1.svg\" alt=\"A legal cut does not yet control height\"><p class=\"v7-print-caption\">The finite increments are (-1,+1,-1,+1,-1), each at least -1 and summing to -1. On 0&lt;=j&lt;5, the minimum -1 is first reached at r=1 and last at r=3. The first cut reaches -1 only at the end; the last cut reaches it at step 3 and fails. Every indexed cut is computed. This is a finite bridge-to-tree-word operation, not itself a standard Brownian excursion. The continuous Vervaat limit also needs a conditioned bridge theorem, cut stability at a unique minimum, and identification of the excursion law. A unary ancestral chain adds a zero-increment prefix but increases all old depths by its length. For l_m=floor(m^(3/4)), l_m/m tends to 0 while l_m/sqrt(m) diverges. This is a deterministic instability example, not a claim that modified trees retain the fixed conditioned GW law or that CRT/GHP convergence is proved.</p></section><section><h4>A legal cut does not yet control height · 2/3</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V53.en.page2.svg\" alt=\"A legal cut does not yet control height\"><p class=\"v7-print-caption\">The finite increments are (-1,+1,-1,+1,-1), each at least -1 and summing to -1. On 0&lt;=j&lt;5, the minimum -1 is first reached at r=1 and last at r=3. The first cut reaches -1 only at the end; the last cut reaches it at step 3 and fails. Every indexed cut is computed. This is a finite bridge-to-tree-word operation, not itself a standard Brownian excursion. The continuous Vervaat limit also needs a conditioned bridge theorem, cut stability at a unique minimum, and identification of the excursion law. A unary ancestral chain adds a zero-increment prefix but increases all old depths by its length. For l_m=floor(m^(3/4)), l_m/m tends to 0 while l_m/sqrt(m) diverges. This is a deterministic instability example, not a claim that modified trees retain the fixed conditioned GW law or that CRT/GHP convergence is proved.</p></section><section><h4>A legal cut does not yet control height · 3/3</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V53.en.page3.svg\" alt=\"A legal cut does not yet control height\"><p class=\"v7-print-caption\">The finite increments are (-1,+1,-1,+1,-1), each at least -1 and summing to -1. On 0&lt;=j&lt;5, the minimum -1 is first reached at r=1 and last at r=3. The first cut reaches -1 only at the end; the last cut reaches it at step 3 and fails. Every indexed cut is computed. This is a finite bridge-to-tree-word operation, not itself a standard Brownian excursion. The continuous Vervaat limit also needs a conditioned bridge theorem, cut stability at a unique minimum, and identification of the excursion law. A unary ancestral chain adds a zero-increment prefix but increases all old depths by its length. For l_m=floor(m^(3/4)), l_m/m tends to 0 while l_m/sqrt(m) diverges. This is a deterministic instability example, not a claim that modified trees retain the fixed conditioned GW law or that CRT/GHP convergence is proved.</p></section></div><output data-v7-result aria-live=\"polite\">r=1 is legal; the bridge endpoint alone does not impose first arrival.</output><details><summary>Assumptions and diagram limits</summary><p>The finite increments are (-1,+1,-1,+1,-1), each at least -1 and summing to -1. On 0&lt;=j&lt;5, the minimum -1 is first reached at r=1 and last at r=3. The first cut reaches -1 only at the end; the last cut reaches it at step 3 and fails. Every indexed cut is computed. This is a finite bridge-to-tree-word operation, not itself a standard Brownian excursion. The continuous Vervaat limit also needs a conditioned bridge theorem, cut stability at a unique minimum, and identification of the excursion law. A unary ancestral chain adds a zero-increment prefix but increases all old depths by its length. For l_m=floor(m^(3/4)), l_m/m tends to 0 while l_m/sqrt(m) diverges. This is a deterministic instability example, not a claim that modified trees retain the fixed conditioned GW law or that CRT/GHP convergence is proved.</p></details><figcaption>An exact finite model reveals the mechanism; full theorems and proofs remain in the text.</figcaption><nav><a href=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume7-v1/V53.en.svg\">SVG</a><a href=\"/print/figures/V53.en.html\">Static print</a></nav></figure>",
      "condition": "The finite increments are (-1,+1,-1,+1,-1), each at least -1 and summing to -1. On 0<=j<5, the minimum -1 is first reached at r=1 and last at r=3. The first cut reaches -1 only at the end; the last cut reaches it at step 3 and fails. Every indexed cut is computed. This is a finite bridge-to-tree-word operation, not itself a standard Brownian excursion. The continuous Vervaat limit also needs a conditioned bridge theorem, cut stability at a unique minimum, and identification of the excursion law. A unary ancestral chain adds a zero-increment prefix but increases all old depths by its length. For l_m=floor(m^(3/4)), l_m/m tends to 0 while l_m/sqrt(m) diverges. This is a deterministic instability example, not a claim that modified trees retain the fixed conditioned GW law or that CRT/GHP convergence is proved.",
